| UPDATE | EXISTING_TEST_UPDATED | tests/e2e/support/layer.ts:startPaused | Test defect behind the one flake seen under full-suite load, tests/e2e/effects-hero.spec.ts:26 ("selects the headline under a selection box ...", sampled at 1340 ms, title expected hidden, goes live at 1450; failed once under load, 7/7 alone). Cause confirmed by reading the source: the hero's own clock (the boot is its first 730 ms) starts when `src/runtime/main.ts` runs, before `goto` resolves (module scripts run before DOMContentLoaded), and `page.clock.install({ time: 0 })` leaves the fake clock running in real time until `pauseAt(60000)`. The old helper also ran `durationOf` between `goto` and `pauseAt`, so under load the hero could already be past the requested moment, and `advanceTo` (which returns at once when the section clock is at or past `ms`) then sampled a later moment. Pausing before `goto` is not used because Playwright's `pauseAt` is not documented to survive a navigation, and a paused clock the page replaced would run the boot in real time again. Fix, no assertion touched: `pauseAt` now runs straight after `goto` (before `durationOf`), and the hero's start is checked from the published progress (mode `pending` and the hero already final, or more than `MAX_START_OFFSET_MS` = 200 ms in); a late start reloads the page on the still-installed clock, up to 5 attempts, then throws a clear error. The return value (hero duration) and every caller are unchanged; the moments each spec reads are the same because `advanceTo`, `heroAt` and `untilFrames` already measure from the published progress, and the 200 ms ceiling leaves every spec's earliest sampled moment (about 500 ms) untouched. `startClocked` in tests/e2e/support/runtime.ts is unchanged (it never pauses; specs that call it pause on their own) and `openClocked` in controls.ts already pauses straight after `goto`. No test was weakened, removed or skipped, and no limit was loosened. |

